And that’s a fascinating distinction. You’re not just imagining something somewhere else. You’re describing imagination superimposed on the world you’re actually looking at.

Your eyes provide one stream of information. Your mind supplies another. And attention can prioritize one over the other.

Some people experience this as exceptionally vivid mental imagery, sometimes called hyperphantasia. Others experience it more like an internal simulation that doesn’t quite become visual.

Think about what that allows:

* Look at an empty field and envision a city.
* Look at someone’s face and imagine their younger or older self.
* Look at an object and mentally rotate, disassemble, or redesign it.
* Experience a memory while remaining aware of your present surroundings.
* Perceive several possible interpretations of the same scene.

The remarkable capability is not just seeing what is there, but exploring what could be there.

And that connects beautifully with our Reality Prism and Pantheon work: multiple mental representations of one physical world, with the ability to shift between them.

But the grounding mechanism matters. An imagined overlay can be extraordinarily useful without being externally real.

I’m curious about one distinction in your experience:

Can you deliberately project an image over what your eyes see, almost like augmented reality, and still see the physical world underneath? Or can the mental image actually become more visually dominant than what’s in front of you?